{# W3C SCXML 6.2: <send>, composed from four parts, each decided once: the
   element's own arguments (the prologue), the payload (`send_event_data`), the
   event, and the delivery. Every delivery arm reads the parts; none evaluates
   anything itself, so an argument cannot be read one way for one target and
   another way for the next. #}
{# W3C SCXML 5.7.1: what a `<param>` that cannot be read does.

   The clause has two halves: `error.execution` goes on the internal queue,
   and the name and value are ignored — the message itself still goes, with
   the pair absent. §6.2's "discard the message" governs `<send>`'s own
   arguments (a namelist entry, an idlocation), not its `<param>` children;
   W3C test343 reads the same clause the same way under `<donedata>`.

   The error was still raised while this element was processed, so the block
   stops after it (§scxml-4.9): `paramFailed` is what the check after the
   message has gone reads.

   A param names its value through `expr` or, failing that, `location`; the
   parser admits exactly one, so an empty value is a `location=""` — a
   location that names nothing, which is the same failure.

   `indent` is the caller's leading whitespace. #}
{% macro param_eval_failed(param, indent) %}
{{ indent }}// W3C SCXML 5.7.1: report the failure and omit the name and value.
{{ indent }}SCE_LOG_ERROR("<send> <param name='{{ param.name | escape_cpp }}'> could not be read");
{{ indent }}engine.raise(typename Engine::EventWithMetadata(
{{ indent }}    Event::Error_execution,
{{ indent }}    "<send> <param name='{{ param.name | escape_cpp }}'> could not be read"));
{{ indent }}paramFailed = true;
{% endmacro %}
{# W3C SCXML 6.2: one of the element's own arguments failed. "If the evaluation
   of <send>'s arguments produces an error, the Processor MUST discard the
   message without attempting to deliver it. Furthermore, it MUST place the
   error 'error.execution' on the internal event queue." The error carries the
   send id (W3C SCXML 5.10.1, test332), and — raised while the element was
   processed — it ends the block (W3C SCXML 4.9). #}
{% macro send_arg_error(message, block_end, indent) %}
{{ indent }}SCE_LOG_ERROR("{{ message | escape_cpp_format }}");
{{ indent }}engine.raise(typename Engine::EventWithMetadata(Event::Error_execution, "{{ message | escape_cpp }}", "", sendId));
{{ indent }}{{ block_end or 'return;' }}  // W3C SCXML 4.9: the error ends the block
{% endmacro %}
{# W3C SCXML 5.10 / B.2: what `_event.data` is for this <send>, computed in ONE
   place. It declares `params` and `typedParams` (the evaluated namelist and
   <param> pairs, text and typed), `eventData` (the text a delivery carries) and
   `typedData` (the typed value, when the payload produced one). Every arm that
   delivers the event reads these, the host-served and BasicHTTP arms
   included.

   A namelist entry is one of the element's own arguments, so one that cannot
   be read discards the message and ends the block.

   `model` and `block_end` are passed in because a macro does not see the
   caller's names. #}
